prompt:
Use Three.js to implement 3D ballistic trajectory visualization and numerical integration, accounting for air resistance and optionally the Coriolis effect.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Place the following importmap in <head> (include it verbatim), and import via ES Modules inside <script type="module">:

<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>

<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
</script>

[Input Controls (Required)]
(1) Launch elevation angle (degrees), id="angle", range 0–90, recommended default value="30";
(2) Initial speed, id="speed", range 10–100 (unit: m/s), recommended default value="95";
(3) Mass, id="mass", range 0.1–10 kg;
(4) id="coriolisToggle": toggle whether Coriolis acceleration is included (the implementation may use the Northern Hemisphere approximation in the form ω×v, with latitude set to a default constant such as 45°);
(5) id="fireBtn" to launch; id="clearBtn" to clear all drawn trajectories;

[Physics Model]
(6) Gravity g=9.81, directed downward;
(7) Air resistance: F_drag = 0.5 * Cd * rho * A * |v|², directed opposite to the velocity; Cd=0.47, rho=1.225 kg/m³; A = π * r²; approximate r from the mass by treating the projectile as a sphere of fixed density: for example, set the sphere density ρ_body=1000 kg/m³ and solve for r from m = (4/3)π r³ ρ_body;
(8) Numerical integration: update velocity and position using a fixed time step or an adaptive small time step until the projectile returns to the ground (y≤0) or execution stops due to a timeout;

[Visualization]
(9) Draw each trajectory as a 3D polyline using THREE.Line or LineSegments, with different colors for different trajectories;
(10) A ground grid or plane at y=0; OrbitControls;
(11) Multiple launches are allowed, and trajectories coexist until cleared;

[HUD (Required)]
(12) id="range": horizontal range (the distance on the XZ plane from the launch point to the landing point);
(13) id="maxHeight": maximum height;
(14) id="flightTime": flight time (seconds);
The three items above must correspond to the most recently completed launch results and be synchronized with __3D_STATE__.

[State Exposure (Required, After Launch and on Every Frame)]
window.__3D_STATE__:
  - trajectoryCount: number of trajectories currently retained
  - angle, speed, mass: consistent with the current parsed control values (number)
  - lastRange, lastMaxHeight, lastFlightTime: results of the most recent launch
  - coriolisEnabled: boolean

Output a single HTML file that runs when opened directly in a browser.
